WEBVTT

00:00.420 --> 00:03.680
In this video, I want to focus
on a few more techniques for

00:03.680 --> 00:05.210
factoring polynomials.

00:05.210 --> 00:09.130
And in particular, I want to
focus on quadratics that don't

00:09.130 --> 00:11.310
have a 1 as the leading
coefficient.

00:11.310 --> 00:15.540
For example, if I wanted
to factor 4x squared

00:15.540 --> 00:20.920
plus 25x minus 21.

00:20.920 --> 00:23.440
Everything we've factored so
far, or all of the quadratics

00:23.440 --> 00:27.100
we've factored so far, had
either a 1 or negative 1 where

00:27.100 --> 00:28.090
this 4 is sitting.

00:28.090 --> 00:30.880
All of a sudden now, we
have this 4 here.

00:30.880 --> 00:32.840
So what I'm going to teach you
is a technique called,

00:32.840 --> 00:35.020
factoring by grouping.

00:35.020 --> 00:37.130
And it's a little bit more
involved than what we've

00:37.130 --> 00:40.100
learned before, but
it's a neat trick.

00:40.100 --> 00:42.210
To some degree, it'll become
obsolete once you learn the

00:42.210 --> 00:44.600
quadratic formula, because,
frankly, the quadratic formula

00:44.600 --> 00:46.230
is a lot easier.

00:46.230 --> 00:47.550
But this is how it goes.

00:47.550 --> 00:48.770
I'll show you the technique.

00:48.770 --> 00:50.910
And then at the end of this
video, I'll actually show you

00:50.910 --> 00:52.300
why it works.

00:52.300 --> 00:55.430
So what we need to do here,
is we need to think of two

00:55.430 --> 01:02.120
numbers, a and b, where a
times b is equal 4 times

01:02.120 --> 01:03.700
negative 21.

01:03.700 --> 01:12.530
So a times b is going to be
equal to 4 times negative 21,

01:12.530 --> 01:15.280
which is equal to negative 84.

01:15.280 --> 01:20.490
And those same two numbers,
a plus b, need

01:20.490 --> 01:24.480
to be equal to 25.

01:24.480 --> 01:25.930
Let me be very clear.

01:25.930 --> 01:29.300
This is the 25, so they need
to be equal to 25.

01:29.300 --> 01:30.970
This is where the 4 is.

01:30.970 --> 01:35.540
So we go, 4 times negative 21.

01:35.540 --> 01:37.270
That's a negative 21.

01:37.270 --> 01:40.680
So what two numbers are there
that would do this?

01:40.680 --> 01:45.050
Well, we have to look at the
factors of negative 84.

01:45.050 --> 01:46.710
And once again, one
of these are going

01:46.710 --> 01:47.550
to have to be positive.

01:47.550 --> 01:50.870
The other ones are going to have
to be negative, because

01:50.870 --> 01:52.170
their product is negative.

01:52.170 --> 01:53.350
So let's think about
the different

01:53.350 --> 01:55.420
factors that might work.

01:55.420 --> 01:59.465
4 and negative 21 look
tantalizing, but when you add

01:59.465 --> 02:01.970
them, you get negative 17.

02:01.970 --> 02:04.870
Or, if you had negative 4 and
21, you'd get positive 17.

02:04.870 --> 02:06.010
Doesn't work.

02:06.010 --> 02:08.220
Let's try some other
combinations.

02:08.220 --> 02:12.150
1 and 84, too far apart when
you take their difference.

02:12.150 --> 02:13.420
Because that's essentially what
you're going to do, if

02:13.420 --> 02:15.500
one is negative and
one is positive.

02:15.500 --> 02:16.840
Too far apart.

02:16.840 --> 02:20.730
Let's see you could do 3--
I'm jumping the gun.

02:20.730 --> 02:23.370
2 and 42.

02:23.370 --> 02:24.620
Once again, too far apart.

02:24.620 --> 02:26.800
Negative 2 plus 42 is 40.

02:26.800 --> 02:29.650
2 plus negative 42 is negative
40-- too far apart.

02:29.650 --> 02:41.020
3 and-- Let's see, 3 goes into
84-- 3 goes into 8 2 times.

02:41.020 --> 02:42.860
2 times 3 is 6.

02:42.860 --> 02:45.200
8 minus 6 is 2.

02:45.200 --> 02:46.610
Bring down the 4.

02:46.610 --> 02:49.150
Goes exactly 8 times.

02:49.150 --> 02:51.120
So 3 and 28.

02:51.120 --> 02:52.370
This seems interesting.

02:57.630 --> 02:59.920
And remember, one of these
has to be negative.

02:59.920 --> 03:06.240
So if we have negative 3 plus
28, that is equal to 25.

03:06.240 --> 03:08.220
Now, we've found our
two numbers.

03:08.220 --> 03:12.200
But it's not going to be quite
as simple of an operation as

03:12.200 --> 03:19.510
what we did when this was
a 1 or negative 1.

03:19.510 --> 03:23.330
What we're going to do now is
split up this term right here.

03:29.160 --> 03:35.800
We're going to split it up into
positive 28x minus 3x.

03:35.800 --> 03:37.590
We're just going to
split that term.

03:37.590 --> 03:39.890
That term is that term
right there.

03:39.890 --> 03:43.150
And of course, you have your
minus 21 there, and you have

03:43.150 --> 03:46.350
your 4x squared over here.

03:46.350 --> 03:49.100
Now, you might say, how did you
pick the 28 to go here,

03:49.100 --> 03:50.570
and the negative
3 to go there?

03:50.570 --> 03:52.000
And it actually does matter.

03:52.000 --> 03:56.730
The way I thought about it is
3 or negative 3, and 21 or

03:56.730 --> 03:59.590
negative 21 , they have
some common factors.

03:59.590 --> 04:02.460
In particular, they have
the factor 3 in common.

04:02.460 --> 04:04.780
And 28 and 4 have some
common factors.

04:04.780 --> 04:07.940
So I grouped the 28 on
the side of the 4.

04:07.940 --> 04:09.730
And you're going to see what
I mean in a second.

04:09.730 --> 04:15.450
If we, literally, group these
so that term becomes 4x

04:15.450 --> 04:18.140
squared plus 28x.

04:18.140 --> 04:25.880
And then, this side, over
here in pink, it's plus

04:25.880 --> 04:28.740
negative 3x minus 21.

04:28.740 --> 04:30.810
Once again, I picked these.

04:30.810 --> 04:34.300
I grouped the negative 3 with
the 21, or the negative 21,

04:34.300 --> 04:36.320
because they're both
divisible by 3.

04:36.320 --> 04:38.790
And I grouped the 28 with the
4, because they're both

04:38.790 --> 04:40.250
divisible by 4.

04:40.250 --> 04:43.800
And now, in each of these
groups, we factor as

04:43.800 --> 04:45.550
much out as we can.

04:45.550 --> 04:49.050
So both of these terms
are divisible by 4x.

04:49.050 --> 04:55.740
So this orange term is equal
to 4x times x-- 4x squared

04:55.740 --> 05:03.020
divided by 4x is just x-- plus
28x divided by 4x is just 7.

05:03.020 --> 05:04.020
Now, this second term.

05:04.020 --> 05:05.630
Remember, you factor
out everything that

05:05.630 --> 05:07.030
you can factor out.

05:07.030 --> 05:10.160
Well, both of these terms are
divisible by 3 or negative 3.

05:10.160 --> 05:11.945
So let's factor out
a negative 3.

05:11.945 --> 05:16.260
And this becomes x plus 7.

05:16.260 --> 05:18.690
And now, something might
pop out at you.

05:18.690 --> 05:25.350
We have x plus 7 times
4x plus, x plus 7

05:25.350 --> 05:27.150
times negative 3.

05:27.150 --> 05:31.630
So we can factor out
an x plus 7.

05:31.630 --> 05:33.550
This might not be completely
obvious.

05:33.550 --> 05:34.900
You're probably not
used to factoring

05:34.900 --> 05:36.490
out an entire binomial.

05:36.490 --> 05:38.350
But you could view this
could be like a.

05:38.350 --> 05:42.790
Or if you have 4xa minus 3a,
you would be able to

05:42.790 --> 05:43.930
factor out an a.

05:43.930 --> 05:46.330
And I can just leave this
as a minus sign.

05:50.150 --> 05:51.950
Let me delete this
plus right here.

05:51.950 --> 05:55.850
Because it's just
minus 3, right?

05:55.850 --> 05:58.970
Plus negative 3, same
thing as minus 3.

05:58.970 --> 06:00.640
So what can we do here?

06:00.640 --> 06:02.270
We have an x plus 7, times 4x.

06:02.270 --> 06:04.040
We have an x plus 7,
times negative 3.

06:04.040 --> 06:06.110
Let's factor out the x plus 7.

06:06.110 --> 06:16.350
We get x plus 7, times
4x minus 3.

06:16.350 --> 06:18.790
Minus that 3 right there.

06:18.790 --> 06:22.110
And we've factored
our binomial.

06:22.110 --> 06:25.550
Sorry, we've factored our
quadratic by grouping.

06:25.550 --> 06:28.210
And we factored it into
two binomials.

06:28.210 --> 06:30.140
Let's do another example of
that, because it's a little

06:30.140 --> 06:30.770
bit involved.

06:30.770 --> 06:34.290
But once you get the hang
of it's kind of fun.

06:34.290 --> 06:43.540
So let's say we want to factor
6x squared plus 7x plus 1.

06:43.540 --> 06:44.800
Same drill.

06:44.800 --> 06:50.940
We want to find a times b that
is equal to 1 times 6, which

06:50.940 --> 06:52.110
is equal to 6.

06:52.110 --> 06:57.460
And we want to find an a plus
b needs to be equal to 7.

06:57.460 --> 06:59.520
This is a little bit more
straightforward.

06:59.520 --> 07:03.900
What are the-- well, the obvious
one is 1 and 6, right?

07:03.900 --> 07:05.310
1 times 6 is 6.

07:05.310 --> 07:07.620
1 plus 6 is 7.

07:07.620 --> 07:10.150
So we have a is equal to 1.

07:10.150 --> 07:11.700
Or let me not even
assign them.

07:11.700 --> 07:15.650
The numbers here are 1 and 6.

07:15.650 --> 07:19.740
Now, we want to split this
into a 1x and a 6x.

07:19.740 --> 07:22.420
But we want to group it so it's
on the side of something

07:22.420 --> 07:24.090
that it shares a factor with.

07:24.090 --> 07:28.880
So we're going to have a 6x
squar ed here, plus-- and so

07:28.880 --> 07:32.520
I'm going to put the
6x first because 6

07:32.520 --> 07:34.170
and 6 share a factor.

07:34.170 --> 07:37.580
And then, we're going to
have plus 1x, right?

07:37.580 --> 07:39.650
6x plus 1x equals 7x .

07:39.650 --> 07:40.830
That was the whole point.

07:40.830 --> 07:42.580
They had to add up to 7 .

07:42.580 --> 07:46.000
And then we have the
final plus 1 there.

07:46.000 --> 07:49.320
Now, in each of these groups,
we can factor out

07:49.320 --> 07:50.220
as much as we like.

07:50.220 --> 07:53.080
So in this first group,
let's factor out a 6x.

07:53.080 --> 07:58.020
So this first group becomes 6x
times-- 6x squar ed divided by

07:58.020 --> 07:59.480
6x is just an x.

07:59.480 --> 08:04.380
6x divided by 6x is just a 1.

08:04.380 --> 08:06.730
And then, the second
group-- we're going

08:06.730 --> 08:08.130
to have a plus here.

08:08.130 --> 08:11.870
But this second group, we just
literally have a x plus 1.

08:11.870 --> 08:16.500
Or we could even write a
1 times an x plus 1.

08:16.500 --> 08:19.440
You could imagine I just
factored out of 1 so to speak.

08:19.440 --> 08:24.790
Now, I have 6x times x plus
1, plus 1 times x plus 1.

08:24.790 --> 08:27.740
Well, I can factor
out the x plus 1.

08:27.740 --> 08:35.549
If I factor out an x plus 1,
that's equal to x plus 1 times

08:35.549 --> 08:37.690
6x plus that 1.

08:37.690 --> 08:38.280
I'm just doing the

08:38.280 --> 08:41.460
distributive property in reverse.

08:41.460 --> 08:43.230
So hopefully you didn't
find that too bad.

08:43.230 --> 08:45.430
And now, I'm going to actually
explain why this little

08:45.430 --> 08:47.170
magical system actually works.

08:50.280 --> 08:52.530
Let me take an example.

08:52.530 --> 08:54.530
I'll do it in very
general terms.

08:54.530 --> 09:03.630
Let's say I had ax plus b,
times cx-- actually, I'm

09:03.630 --> 09:05.300
afraid to use the a's and b's.

09:05.300 --> 09:07.760
I think that'll confuse
you, because I

09:07.760 --> 09:08.930
use a's and b's here.

09:08.930 --> 09:10.240
They won't be the same thing.

09:10.240 --> 09:13.760
So let me use completely
different letters.

09:13.760 --> 09:24.000
Let's say I have fx plus g,
times hx plus, I'll use j

09:24.000 --> 09:25.240
instead of i.

09:25.240 --> 09:26.450
You'll learn in the future
why don't like

09:26.450 --> 09:28.710
using i as a variable.

09:28.710 --> 09:31.000
So what is this going
to be equal to?

09:31.000 --> 09:35.920
Well, it's going to be fx
times hx which is fhx.

09:35.920 --> 09:37.710
And then, fx times j.

09:37.710 --> 09:41.800
So plus fjx.

09:41.800 --> 09:45.240
And then, we're going
to have g times hx.

09:45.240 --> 09:49.010
So plus ghx.

09:49.010 --> 09:50.990
And then g times j.

09:50.990 --> 09:53.840
Plus gj.

09:53.840 --> 10:02.500
Or, if we add these two middle
terms, you have fh times x,

10:02.500 --> 10:08.970
plus-- add these two terms--
fj plus gh x.

10:08.970 --> 10:12.170
Plus gj.

10:12.170 --> 10:14.540
Now, what did I do here?

10:14.540 --> 10:17.550
Well, remember, in all of these
problems where you have

10:17.550 --> 10:20.650
a non-1 or non-negative 1
coefficient here, we look for

10:20.650 --> 10:23.560
two numbers that add up to this,
whose product is equal

10:23.560 --> 10:25.480
to the product of
that times that.

10:25.480 --> 10:31.780
Well, here we have two numbers
that add up-- let's say that a

10:31.780 --> 10:33.030
is equal to fj.

10:36.970 --> 10:37.750
That is a.

10:37.750 --> 10:40.930
And b is equal to gh.

10:40.930 --> 10:43.730
So a plus b is going to be
equal to that middle

10:43.730 --> 10:44.980
coefficient.

10:48.370 --> 10:52.100
And then what is a times b? a
times b is going to be equal

10:52.100 --> 10:56.260
to fj times gh.

10:59.140 --> 11:01.320
We could just reorder these
terms. We're just multiplying

11:01.320 --> 11:04.430
a bunch of terms. So that could
be rewritten as f times

11:04.430 --> 11:09.390
h times g times j.

11:09.390 --> 11:11.090
These are all the same things.

11:11.090 --> 11:14.620
Well, what is fh times gj?

11:14.620 --> 11:19.150
This is equal to fh times gj.

11:19.150 --> 11:22.530
Well, this is equal to the first
coefficient times the

11:22.530 --> 11:23.670
constant term.

11:23.670 --> 11:27.870
So a plus b will be equal to
the middle coefficient.

11:27.870 --> 11:31.930
And a times b will equal the
first coefficient times the

11:31.930 --> 11:32.670
constant term.

11:32.670 --> 11:37.330
So that's why this whole
factoring by grouping even

11:37.330 --> 11:40.180
works, or how we're able
to figure out what

11:40.180 --> 11:42.200
a and b even are.

11:42.200 --> 11:44.210
Now, I'm going to close up
with something slightly

11:44.210 --> 11:45.800
different, but just to make
sure that you have a

11:45.800 --> 11:49.130
well-rounded education
in factoring things.

11:49.130 --> 11:52.050
What I want to do is to teach
you to factor things a little

11:52.050 --> 11:52.940
bit more completely.

11:52.940 --> 11:55.160
And this is a little
bit of a add-on.

11:55.160 --> 11:56.660
I was going to make a
whole video on this.

11:56.660 --> 11:59.250
But I think, on some level,
it might be a

11:59.250 --> 12:00.480
little obvious for you.

12:00.480 --> 12:07.190
So let's say we had-- let
me get a good one here.

12:07.190 --> 12:12.910
Let's say we had negative
x to the third, plus 17x

12:12.910 --> 12:18.180
squared, minus 70x.

12:18.180 --> 12:20.130
Immediately, you say, gee, this
isn't even a quadratic.

12:20.130 --> 12:21.770
I don't know how to solve
something like this.

12:21.770 --> 12:23.580
It has an x to third power.

12:23.580 --> 12:26.150
And the first thing you should
realize is that every term

12:26.150 --> 12:27.840
here is divisible by x.

12:27.840 --> 12:29.920
So let's factor out an x.

12:29.920 --> 12:32.260
Or even better, let's factor
out a negative x.

12:32.260 --> 12:35.320
So if you factor out a negative
x, this is equal to

12:35.320 --> 12:38.730
negative x times-- negative
x to the third divided by

12:38.730 --> 12:41.810
negative x is x squared.

12:41.810 --> 12:47.910
17x squared divided by negative
x is negative 17x.

12:47.910 --> 12:52.510
Negative 70x divided by negative
x is positive 70.

12:52.510 --> 12:54.060
The x's cancel out.

12:54.060 --> 12:56.050
And now, you have something
that might look

12:56.050 --> 12:59.220
a little bit familiar.

12:59.220 --> 13:02.530
We have just a standard
quadratic where the leading

13:02.530 --> 13:03.650
coefficient is a 1.

13:03.650 --> 13:08.290
We just have to find two numbers
whose product is 70,

13:08.290 --> 13:11.000
and that add up to
negative 17.

13:11.000 --> 13:12.920
And the numbers that immediately
jumped into my

13:12.920 --> 13:17.410
head are negative 10
and negative 7.

13:17.410 --> 13:19.520
You take their product,
you get 70.

13:19.520 --> 13:21.870
You add them up, you
get negative 17.

13:21.870 --> 13:25.920
So this part right here is
going to be x minus 10,

13:25.920 --> 13:28.410
times x minus 7.

13:28.410 --> 13:31.540
And of course, you have that
leading negative x.

13:31.540 --> 13:34.330
The general idea here is just
see if there's anything you

13:34.330 --> 13:35.320
can factor out.

13:35.320 --> 13:37.780
And that'll get it into a form
that you might recognize.

13:37.780 --> 13:38.950
Hopefully, you found
this helpful.

13:38.950 --> 13:41.145
I want to reiterate what I
showed you at the beginning of

13:41.145 --> 13:41.510
this video.

13:41.510 --> 13:44.960
I think it's a really cool
trick, so to speak, to be able

13:44.960 --> 13:49.470
to factor things that have
a non-1 or non-negative 1

13:49.470 --> 13:50.460
leading coefficient.

13:50.460 --> 13:52.720
But to some degree, you're going
to find out easier ways

13:52.720 --> 13:54.370
to do this, especially
with the quadratic

13:54.370 --> 13:56.620
formula, in not too long.
